{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>Stretch out your hand, etc., and let the waters return, etc.</b> One needs to know for what purpose it was necessary to stretch out his hand for the returning of the waters — would they not of themselves not persist in doing so, and upon the completion of Israel's crossing, which is also the completion of the Egyptians' final descent, the sea would return of itself?" "Or it may be that because of the very reason itself — that it remained split also in the places where Israel had already passed, and the Egyptians were coming there — the sea reasoned: 'what of these, so too of these' [i.e., just as Israel is unworthy, so too the Egyptians], and HaShem decreed it so in order that they should all enter together into the sea. And this reason was not known to the sea, nor did it entertain a thought contrary to its Master's; therefore HaShem commanded Moses to signal to the sea that its mission was complete and it should go according to its customary way." "<b>Further,</b> it may be explained by way of the statement of them of blessed memory in tractate Yom Tov (Beitzah 5a): 'Any matter that was established by a quorum requires another quorum to annul it.' And they proved this from what the verse says (Deuteronomy 5:27): ״שׁוּבוּ לָכֶם לְאָהֳלֵיכֶם״ — even though there is no reason to prohibit further, nevertheless it was necessary to permit." "<b>Further,</b> the reason for the stretching out: because without this, although the sea would return, it would return slowly, gradually, because its strength had been weakened by the change that had come upon it — and thereby they could escape. Therefore HaShem commanded Moses to rouse the sea, through the stretching out of his hand, to return to its original strength immediately and instantly, and so it says <b>and it returned</b>, etc., <b>to its strength (לְאֵיתָנוֹ)</b>. Or it may be that since the sea was struck by the hand of Moses, HaShem desired that through Moses it should return and be healed, through the stretching out of his hand, with HaShem supporting it, as it says (Isaiah 63:12): ״מוֹלִיךְ לִימִין מֹשֶׁה זְרוֹעַ תִּפְאַרְתּוֹ״, may He be blessed." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by the stretching out of his hand to signal to the sea that it should do its work upon them completely — that no horse or its rider should escape, and that it should not refrain from them in any manner, so that no Egyptian should manage to ascend from the sea. And as for those at the edges of the sea who in a single stroke turned away from the sea — He granted the sea authority over all of them, so that not one of them should escape, and so it says <b>and the waters returned and covered</b>, etc."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:28:11.342726146Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}